    "compose_patient": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You were in hospital after a road traffic collision at around 40mph. You were the driver and were wearing a seatbelt.\n\nThe team found:\n- A broken left shin bone (closed left tibial shaft fracture)\n- A tear to your spleen (grade II splenic laceration)\n- Broken ribs on the left side, ribs 7 to 9, with a small collection of blood around the lung (haemothorax)\n- Small cracks in a bone in your lower back (undisplaced L1 transverse process fractures)"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "You had scans of your chest, abdomen, pelvis and spine. Your pelvis and spine were otherwise clear on imaging.\n\nFor your broken shin bone, the team performed an operation on Day 2 (13/05) to fix it with a metal rod inside the bone (intramedullary nailing).\n\nYour spleen injury was treated without an operation. The team monitored you closely and checked your blood levels. Repeat scans showed the spleen improving.\n\nA tube (chest drain) was put in by the respiratory and thoracics team to drain the blood from around your lung. Your lung re-expanded. The drain was removed on Day 4 (15/05), and a chest X-ray after removal was satisfactory.\n\nThe physiotherapy team helped you to start moving. You are putting partial weight on your left leg, as advised by the orthopaedic team."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7"
          ],
          "text": "- You have a follow-up in the orthopaedic fracture clinic at 2 weeks.\n- You have a telephone review with the general surgery team about your spleen at 6 weeks. Advice about splenic vaccination will be given at this review.\n- The respiratory and thoracics team do not need to see you routinely."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.4",
          "heading": "safety_net",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{SAFETY_NET}}"
        }
